    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of the change implementing soft-delete for owners.\n\n## What changed\n\n**Soft-delete on DELETE** (`DeleteOwner.java`): instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`, it now sets `deleted = true` and saves, retaining the row. The `DELETE /api/owners/{id}` pipeline still responds 204 (2xx).\n\n**New `deleted` field:**\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added a nullable `Boolean deleted` column with getter/setter.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` response schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it.\n- `OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 maps `deleted`, coercing null\u2192false (mirroring `bulkSignupWarning`/`possibleDuplicate`), so a freshly created owner reads back `false` and a soft-deleted one reads `true`.\n- Schema files for all four DBs (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `deleted` column.\n\n**Create-path duplicate/identity checks ignore deleted owners:**\n- `RequireUniqueIdentity.java` \u2014 the household-identity block skips soft-deleted owners, so a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only match is deleted (the 409 no longer fires).\n- `AssignPossibleDuplicate.java` \u2014 the soft possible-duplicate flag also skips deleted owners.\n\n## Verification\n- `coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains`: new owner \u2192 `deleted:false`; after DELETE, GET still returns the owner with `deleted:true`. \u2705\n- `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck`: create \u2192 soft-delete \u2192 recreate the same owner succeeds (2xx). \u2705\n- Full suite: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nOne scoping note: I deliberately left the count/quota rules (`AssignNamesakeCount`, `AssignHouseholdSize`, `RequireCityCapacity`, `RequireDailyLimit`) unchanged \u2014 the spec calls out \"duplicate and identity checks,\" which are `RequireUniqueIdentity` (the blocking identity/duplicate check) and `AssignPossibleDuplicate` (the possible-duplicate check). The JDBC repository impl (inactive profile; tests use `spring-data-jpa`) was also left as-is since its column-scoped SQL is outside the tested scope.",
